ebruijn-linearize|debruijn-register-compile|linearize|code)\\s\"\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_register_benchmark_test.cljc:19:            [yin.vm.debruijn-linearize :as linearize]\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_register_benchmark_test.cljc:21:            [yin.vm.debruijn-register-compile :as register-compile]\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_register_benchmark_test.cljc:22:            [yin.vm.debruijn-resolve :as resolve]\ntest/yang/python/antlr/int_contract_test.cljc:22:    [yin.vm.debruijn-linearize :as dl]\ntest/yang/python/antlr/int_contract_test.cljc:23:    [yin.vm.debruijn-register-compile :as rc]\ntest/yang/python/antlr/int_contract_test.cljc:172:              (vm/run (dvm/create-vm (:image (dl/adapt (vm/ast->datoms ast)))\ntest/yang/python/antlr/int_contract_test.cljc:179:                     (:image (rc/adapt (vm/ast->datoms ast)))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc:45:   use; B2 (`yin.vm.debruijn-linearize`) produces real images against this\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc:922:             \"The de Bruijn kernel executes raw instruction vectors, not AST: lower and adapt it first (yin.vm.debruijn-linearize/adapt), then load and run the resulting image\"\ntest/yin/vm/engine_gate_test.cljc:10:            [yin.vm.debruijn-linearize :as dl]\ntest/yin/vm/engine_gate_test.cljc:11:            [yin.vm.debruijn-register-compile :as rc]\ntest/yin/vm/engine_gate_test.cljc:432:     (vm/run (cond-> (dvm/create-vm (:image (dl/adapt (vm/ast->datoms ast)))\ntest/yin/vm/engine_gate_test.cljc:439:     (vm/run (cond-> (rvm/create-vm (:image (rc/adapt (second\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc:855:             \"(yin.vm.debruijn-register-compile/adapt), then load and run the \"\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_resolve.cljc:1:(ns yin.vm.debruijn-resolve\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linearize.cljc:1:(ns yin.vm.debruijn-linearize\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linearize.cljc:3:   `lower-stack` consumes `yin.vm.debruijn-resolve/resolve`'s output\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linearize.cljc:18:   `yin.vm.debruijn-code`, and `yin.vm.debruijn-resolve`.\"\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linearize.cljc:22:            [yin.vm.debruijn-resolve :as resolve]))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linearize.cljc:213:  \"`{:keys [tuples source params]}` (`yin.vm.debruijn-resolve/resolve`'s\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linearize.cljc:218:   Calls `yin.vm.debruijn-resolve/validate-resolved` first and refuses on\ntest/yang/python/antlr/int_heap_test.cljc:14:    [yin.vm.debruijn-linearize :as dl]\ntest/yang/python/antlr/int_heap_test.cljc:15:    [yin.vm.debruijn-register-compile :as rc]\ntest/yang/python/antlr/int_heap_test.cljc:81:   :stack {:image (fn [ast] (:image (dl/adapt (vm/ast->datoms ast)))),\ntest/yang/python/antlr/int_heap_test.cljc:86:                       (:image (rc/adapt (second (vm/ast->datoms-with-root\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc:14:   layout `yin.vm.debruijn-linearize/lower-stack` already uses for the\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc:294:;; Used by both `yin.vm.debruijn-register-compile/lower-register`, to\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc:299:;; (`yin.vm.debruijn-register-compile/allocate-temp!`/`free-temp!`) is a\ntest/yang/python/antlr/prelude_parity_test.cljc:26:    [yin.vm.debruijn-linearize :as dl]\ntest/yang/python/antlr/prelude_parity_test.cljc:27:    [yin.vm.debruijn-register-compile :as rc]\ntest/yang/python/antlr/int_ops_test.cljc:12:    [yin.vm.debruijn-linearize :as dl]\ntest/yang/python/antlr/int_ops_test.cljc:13:    [yin.vm.debruijn-register-compile :as rc]\ntest/yang/python/antlr/int_ops_test.cljc:52:              (vm/run (dvm/create-vm (:image (dl/adapt (vm/ast->datoms ast)))\ntest/yang/python/antlr/int_ops_test.cljc:59:                     (:image (rc/adapt (vm/ast->datoms ast)))\ntest/yang/python/antlr/int_literal_test.cljc:28:    [yin.vm.debruijn-linearize :as dl]\ntest/yang/python/antlr/int_literal_test.cljc:29:    [yin.vm.debruijn-register-compile :as rc]\ntest/yang/python/antlr/int_literal_test.cljc:451:              (vm/run (dvm/create-vm (:image (dl/adapt (vm/ast->datoms ast)))\ntest/yang/python/antlr/int_literal_test.cljc:458:                     (:image (rc/adapt (vm/ast->datoms ast)))\ntest/yang/python/antlr/e2e_c2_test.clj:18:    [yin.vm.debruijn-linearize :as dl]\ntest/yang/python/antlr/e2e_c2_test.clj:19:    [yin.vm.debruijn-register-compile :as rc]\ntest/yang/python/antlr/e2e_c2_test.clj:1150:            (vm/run (dvm/create-vm (:image (dl/adapt datoms))\ntest/yang/python/antlr/e2e_c2_test.clj:1153:               (vm/run (rvm/create-vm (:image (rc/adapt datoms))\n(ns yin.vm.debruijn-vm-contract-test\n  \"B0 (docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md, 'B0: contract and normalizer'):\n   freezes the result/error normalizer and the parity corpus that later\n   phases (B1+) depend on. Adds no evaluator, no AST, no dimension -- it\n   only pins tools that already exist.\n\n   The normalizer wraps `yin.vm.parity-test/normalize`, which already\n   strips a closure's host environment and reduces a stream-ref to its id;\n   this namespace extends that with the design's arity-only closure rule,\n   the cursor-ref-by-id rule, and the type-only continuation rule (S1,\n   'Architecture and invariants'), and applies the whole rule set\n   recursively with the same traversal `yin.vm/strip-reader-positions`\n   already uses, so a store snapshot or an ex-data map normalizes its\n   nested values too.\n\n   The corpus is `yin.vm.parity-test/corpus` (the v2 parity corpus) plus\n   `yin.vm-test/semantic-bytecode-corpus` (mint_test's own every-tag\n   reuse target, already proved by `yin.vm-test/semantic-bytecode-corpus-\n   covers-every-tag` to cover every `semantic-bytecode-grammar` tag).\n   `yin.vm.completion-test`'s own extra fixtures (`scoping-corpus`,\n   `kitchen-sink`, `maker-program`, and `conformance-corpus` itself) are\n   `^:private` and the file box for this phase forbids editing that\n   namespace to expose them, so they are not required here; the coverage\n   assertion below still holds because the reused corpus alone already\n   spans every tag.\"\n  (:require [clojure.test :refer [deftest is testing]]\n            [yin.vm :as vm]\n            [yin.vm.ast-walker :as ast-walker]\n            [yin.vm.completion :as completion]\n            [yin.vm.parity-test :as parity]\n            [yin.vm.values :as values]\n            [yin.vm.test-utils :as tu]\n            [yin.vm-test :as vm-test]))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The normalizer\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- normalize-node\n  \"One node of the design's B0 normalizer: reduces a closure to its arity,\n   a cursor reference to its id, and a parked or reified continuation to\n   its bare type; everything else -- including the stream-ref-by-id and\n   host-fn rules -- defers to `parity/normalize`, reused rather than\n   reimplemented.\"\n  [value]\n  (cond\n    ;; a host-typed closure or continuation normalizes as its payload\n    (values/host-typed? value) (normalize-node (values/payload value))\n\n    (and (map? value) (= :closure (:type value)))\n    {:type :closure,\n     :arity (if (contains? value :arity) (:arity value) (count (:params value)))}\n\n    (and (map? value) (= :cursor-ref (:type value)))\n    {:type :cursor-ref, :id (:id value)}\n\n    (and (map? value)\n         (contains? #{:parked-continuation :reified-continuation} (:type value)))\n    {:type (:type value)}\n\n    :else (parity/normalize value)))\n\n\n(defn normalize\n  \"B0's frozen result/error normalizer (design doc S1): a value comparable\n   independent of host or object identity. Depth-first, transforming\n   children before the collection itself -- the same traversal shape as\n   `yin.vm/strip-reader-positions`, reused here for a different rewrite --\n   so a normalized closure, continuation, or stream/cursor reference nested\n   inside a store snapshot or an ex-data map is caught too.\"\n  [value]\n  (let [value' (cond (record? value) value\n                     (map? value) (into (empty value)\n                                        (map (fn [[k v]] [(normalize k) (normalize v)]))\n                                        value)\n                     (set? value) (into (empty value) (map normalize) value)\n                     (vector? value) (mapv normalize value)\n                     (sequential? value) (apply list (map normalize value))\n                     :else value)]\n    (normalize-node value')))\n\n\n(defn normalize-error\n  \"The design's error rule (S1): the message, plus ex-data with the value\n   normalizer recursively applied to every value it holds.\"\n  [ex]\n  {:message (ex-message ex), :data (normalize (ex-data ex))})\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The corpus\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Reused, not duplicated: the v2 parity corpus and the codec's every-tag\n;; corpus, both already public and already exercised elsewhere for exactly\n;; this purpose (mint_test.cljc requires both under the same names).\n\n(def ^:private tag-corpus\n  \"Every AST the emitter's grammar defines a tag for, reused from\n   `yin.vm-test/semantic-bytecode-corpus` -- mint_test's own reuse\n   target for the same claim.\"\n  vm-test/semantic-bytecode-corpus)\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Helpers for the fixtures this namespace adds\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- lit\n  [v]\n  {:type :literal, :value v})\n\n\n(defn- variable\n  [n]\n  {:type :variable, :name n})\n\n\n(defn- lambda\n  [params body]\n  {:type :lambda, :params params, :body body})\n\n\n(defn- app\n  [f & args]\n  {:type :application, :operator f, :operands (vec args)})\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; 1. Coverage: every tag the reused corpora exercise appears here\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(deftest all-node-fixture-coverage-test\n  (testing \"the reused every-tag corpus plus the v2 parity corpus together\n            exercise every semantic-bytecode tag -- the same technique\n            `yin.vm-test/semantic-bytecode-corpus-covers-every-tag` already\n            uses to prove this of `tag-corpus` alone\"\n    (is (= (set (keys vm/semantic-bytecode-grammar))\n           (into #{}\n                 (comp (mapcat (comp vals :rows vm/ast->semantic-bytecode))\n                       (map second))\n                 (concat tag-corpus (map second parity/corpus)))))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; 2. Named-VM self-parity\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(deftest named-vm-self-parity-test\n  (testing \"every parity-corpus program normalizes the same across two\n            independent named-VM runs\"\n    (doseq [[name ast _expected] parity/corpus]\n      (testing name\n        (is (= (normalize (tu/compile-and-run ast))\n               (normalize (tu/compile-and-run ast)))))))\n  (testing \"the walker fed on a datom batch agrees with the walker fed on\n            rows, the same two paths `parity-test/ast-walker-parity-test`\n            already compares\"\n    (doseq [[name ast _expected] parity/corpus]\n      (testing name\n        (let [via-batch (normalize (vm/value (vm/eval (tu/create-vm) ast)))\n              via-rows (normalize\n                         (vm/value\n                           (vm/run\n                             (ast-walker/vm-load-rows\n                               (tu/create-vm)\n                               (vm/ast->semantic-bytecode ast)\n                               vm/ast-contract))))]\n          (is (= via-batch via-rows)))))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; 3. Idempotence of the normalizer\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(def ^:private idempotence-fixtures\n  [42\n   \"hello\"\n   nil\n   [1 2 3]\n   {:x 10, :y 20}\n   #{1 2}\n   {:type :closure, :params '[x y], :body (variable 'x), :env {'+ +}}\n   {:type :stream-ref, :id :stream-3}\n   {:type :cursor-ref, :id :cursor-1}\n   {:type :parked-continuation, :id :parked-2, :k nil, :env {}}\n   {:type :reified-continuation, :k nil, :env {'x 1}}\n   +\n   {:store/a {:type :closure, :params '[x], :body (variable 'x), :env {}},\n    :store/b {:type :stream-ref, :id :stream-0},\n    :store/c 7}])\n\n\n(deftest normalizer-idempotence-test\n  (doseq [value idempotence-fixtures]\n    (testing (pr-str value)\n      (is (= (normalize value) (normalize (normalize value)))))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; 4. Normalized comparisons per category\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(deftest closure-normalization-test\n  (let [ast (lambda '[x] (variable 'x))\n        run1 (normalize (vm/value (vm/eval (tu/create-vm) ast)))\n        run2 (normalize (vm/value (vm/eval (tu/create-vm) ast)))]\n    (is (= {:type :closure, :arity 1} run1) \"arity only, no params or body\")\n    (is (= run1 run2) \"self-parity\")))\n\n\n(deftest continuation-normalization-test\n  (testing \"a reified continuation (:vm/current-continuation)\"\n    (let [ast {:type :vm/current-continuation}\n          run1 (normalize (vm/value (vm/eval (tu/create-vm) ast)))\n          run2 (normalize (vm/value (vm/eval (tu/create-vm) ast)))]\n      (is (= {:type :reified-continuation} run1) \"type only, no k or env\")\n      (is (= run1 run2) \"self-parity\")))\n  (testing \"a parked continuation (:vm/park), UCF-shaped per\n            yin.vm.completion\"\n    (let [ast {:type :vm/park}\n          run1 (normalize (vm/value (vm/eval (tu/create-vm) ast)))\n          run2 (normalize (vm/value (vm/eval (tu/create-vm) ast)))]\n      (is (= {:type :parked-continuation} run1) \"type only, no id, k or env\")\n      (is (= run1 run2) \"self-parity\"))))\n\n\n(deftest error-normalization-test\n  (let [ast (app (lit 1) (lit 2))\n        catch-normalized\n        (fn []\n          (try\n            (vm/eval (tu/create-vm) ast)\n            ::no-error\n            (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Exception :cljs :default) e\n              (normalize-error e))))\n        run1 (catch-normalized)\n        run2 (catch-normalized)]\n    (is (= \"Cannot apply non-function\" (:message run1)))\n    (is (= {:reason :not-applicable, :kind :number} (:data run1))\n        \"a qualified refusal: the operator's kind, never the value\")\n    (is (= run1 run2) \"self-parity\")))\n\n\n(deftest stream-normalization-test\n  (let [ast {:type :stream/make, :buffer 4}\n        run1 (normalize (vm/value (vm/eval (tu/create-vm) ast)))\n        run2 (normalize (vm/value (vm/eval (tu/create-vm) ast)))]\n    (is (= {:type :stream-ref, :id :stream-0} run1) \"by identity, as parity/normalize already reduces it\")\n    (is (= run1 run2) \"self-parity\")))\n\n\n(deftest cursor-normalization-test\n  (let [make-cursor (fn []\n                      (let [vm0 (vm/eval (tu/create-vm) {:type :stream/make, :buffer 4})\n                            sref (vm/value vm0)\n                            vm1 (vm/eval vm0 {:type :stream/cursor, :source (lit sref)})]\n                        (normalize (vm/value vm1))))\n        run1 (make-cursor)\n        run2 (make-cursor)]\n    (is (= :cursor-ref (:type run1)))\n    (is (= run1 run2) \"self-parity across two fresh VMs\")))\n\n\n(deftest store-normalization-test\n  (let [ast {:type :vm/store-put, :key :b0/k, :val {:type :literal, :ignored true}}\n        make-store (fn []\n                     (let [vm (vm/eval (tu/create-vm) ast)]\n                       ;; host handles and telemetry are excluded (S1):\n                       ;; `completion/ffi-pair-keys` already names the\n                       ;; default VM's own call-in/call-out/cursor keys,\n                       ;; the same set `completion-test` excludes from a\n                       ;; store slice.\n                       (normalize (apply dissoc (vm/store vm) completion/ffi-pair-keys))))\n        run1 (make-store)\n        run2 (make-store)]\n    (is (= {:type :literal, :ignored true} (:b0/k run1))\n        \"a plain-data store value normalizes unchanged\")\n    (is (= run1 run2) \"self-parity\")))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; 5. Duplicate parameters\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(deftest duplicate-parameter-normalization-test\n  (testing \"rightmost binding wins (engine/bind-params zips params over\n            args into a map, so a repeated name keeps its last value), and\n            the two normalized runs still agree\"\n    (let [ast (app (lambda '[x x] (variable 'x)) (lit 1) (lit 2))\n          run1 (normalize (vm/value (vm/eval (tu/create-vm) ast)))\n          run2 (normalize (vm/value (vm/eval (tu/create-vm) ast)))]\n      (is (= 2 run1))\n      (is (= run1 run2))))\n  (testing \"the closure itself, unapplied, still normalizes to its arity\n            with the duplicate name collapsed by nothing -- arity counts\n            declared params, not distinct names\"\n    (let [ast (lambda '[x x] (variable 'x))\n          run1 (normalize (vm/value (vm/eval (tu/create-vm) ast)))]\n      (is (= {:type :closure, :arity 2} run1)))))\n(ns yin.vm.debruijn-register-contract-test\n  \"R0 (docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md, 'R0: contract and\n   corpus'): freezes the register descriptor's contract version, the\n   operand mapping every resolved node type gets under section 4.4, the\n   two-way address law over the reused B0/B2 corpus, and a set of pure\n   data checks on the descriptor's own field list (section 3).\n\n   This phase builds no register lowerer, no register VM, and no\n   `:yin.debruijn.register/*` image. It reads only already-merged code:\n   `yin.vm.debruijn-resolve/resolve` (B2) and\n   `yin.vm.debruijn-linearize/lower-stack`/`adapt` (B2). Everything below\n   is data and tests against that code, not a new evaluator.\n\n   DECISION (design section 8, DECIDED item 1, quoted): 'R0-R2 are\n   lowerer and format work; R4 is gated by R3.' This file does not\n   assume, import, construct, or exercise a register kernel. There is no\n   `yin.vm.debruijn.register` namespace yet, and nothing here implies one\n   is coming; a register kernel is authorized only if R3's benchmark\n   shows a material benefit over the committed stack VM (design section\n   1 and section 6's R3/R4 boxes).\"\n  (:require [clojure.test :refer [deftest is testing]]\n            [yin.vm :as vm]\n            [yin.vm.debruijn-linearize :as dl]\n            [yin.vm.debruijn-resolve :as dr]\n            [yin.vm.parity-test :as parity]))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; 1. The register descriptor's contract version\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(def register-contract-version\n  \"4, the version of the `:yin.debruijn.register/*` descriptor's\n   contract (the \\\"r2\\\" contract, Rule R's `:define`). Bumped only when\n   register opcode shape, allocation, scalar framing, or control-flow\n   rules change.\"\n  4)\n\n\n(deftest register-contract-version-is-a-frozen-positive-integer\n  (is (integer? register-contract-version))\n  (is (pos? register-contract-version))\n  (is (= 4 register-contract-version) \"Rule R version\"))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; 2. The operand mapping (design section 4.4)\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Every node type `yin.vm.debruijn-resolve/resolve`'s own `case` dispatch\n;; in `emit-node!` handles, read from that file directly (its file box\n;; forbids editing that namespace to export this list, so this vocabulary\n;; is this phase's own frozen copy, not a derived value).\n\n(def resolver-node-type-vocabulary\n  \"The complete `:yin/type` vocabulary `yin.vm.debruijn-resolve/resolve`\n   can produce a resolved record for, per its `emit-node!` case dispatch.\"\n  #{:literal :variable :lambda :application :dao.stream.apply/call :if\n    :vm/gensym :vm/store-get :vm/store-put :stream/make :stream/put\n    :stream/cursor :stream/next :stream/close :vm/park\n    :vm/current-continuation :vm/resume})\n\n\n(def register-instruction-mnemonics\n  \"Section 4.4's register instruction set, as data, so the operand\n   mapping below can be checked against it without hand-copying mnemonic\n   spellings into each entry's assertion.\"\n  #{:const :load-bound :load-free :closure :move :call :branch-false\n    :jump :return :halt :store-get :store-put :gensym :stream-make\n    :stream-put :stream-cursor :stream-next :stream-close :ffi-call\n    :current-continuation :park :resume})\n\n\n(def node-type->register-mapping\n  \"For every resolved-tuple node type in `resolver-node-type-vocabulary`,\n   which register instruction shape from section 4.4's table it maps to.\n   `:variable` carries two shapes under one key because its register\n   mapping depends on the resolution it already carries (bound vs free),\n   not on its node type alone.\"\n  {:literal\n   {:register-instructions [:const], :shape \"[op rd value]\"}\n\n   :variable\n   {:register-instructions [:load-bound :load-free]\n    :bound {:instruction :load-bound, :shape \"[op rd depth position]\"}\n    :free {:instruction :load-free, :shape \"[op rd name]\"}}\n\n   :lambda\n   {:register-instructions [:closure], :shape \"[op rd arity body-pc]\"}\n\n   :application\n   {:register-instructions [:call]\n    :shape \"[op rd fn-reg arg-regs tail? live]\"}\n\n   :if\n   {:register-instructions [:branch-false :jump]\n    :shape \"[op cond-reg target] then [op target]; both arms write the\n             expression's one destination register (design section 4.3)\"}\n\n   :dao.stream.apply/call\n   {:register-instructions [:ffi-call]\n    :shape \"[op rd ffi-op arg-regs live]\"}\n\n   :stream/make\n   {:register-instructions [:stream-make], :shape \"[op rd capacity]\"}\n\n   :stream/put\n   {:register-instructions [:stream-put]\n    :shape \"[op rd stream-reg value-reg live]\"}\n\n   :stream/cursor\n   {:register-instructions [:stream-cursor], :shape \"[op rd stream-reg]\"}\n\n   :stream/next\n   {:register-instructions [:stream-next]\n    :shape \"[op rd cursor-reg live]\"}\n\n   :stream/close\n   {:register-instructions [:stream-close], :shape \"[op rd stream-reg]\"}\n\n   :vm/gensym\n   {:register-instructions [:gensym], :shape \"[op rd prefix]\"}\n\n   :vm/store-get\n   {:register-instructions [:store-get], :shape \"[op rd key]\"}\n\n   :vm/store-put\n   {:register-instructions [:store-put], :shape \"[op rd key value]\"}\n\n   :vm/park\n   {:register-instructions [:park], :shape \"[op rd live]\"}\n\n   :vm/resume\n   {:register-instructions [:resume], :shape \"[op parked-id value-reg]\"}\n\n   :vm/current-continuation\n   {:register-instructions [:current-continuation], :shape \"[op rd live]\"}})\n\n\n(deftest operand-mapping-covers-every-resolver-node-type-exactly\n  (testing \"no node type is silently unaccounted for\"\n    (is (= resolver-node-type-vocabulary\n           (set (keys node-type->register-mapping))))))\n\n\n(deftest all-mappings-name-only-section-four-four-instructions\n  (doseq [[type mapping] node-type->register-mapping]\n    (testing type\n      (is (every? register-instruction-mnemonics\n                  (:register-instructions mapping))))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; 3. The two-way address law (design section 2.2)\n;; =============================================================================\n;; addresses(resolve x) = addresses(lower-stack (resolve x)), over the\n;; reused B0 parity corpus plus B2's duplicate-parameter and shared-\n;; occurrence fixtures. This is the baseline R1 extends with a third,\n;; register-image sequence once a register lowerer exists; neither side\n;; below reaches into a register lowerer, since none exists yet.\n\n(defn- ast-datoms\n  [ast]\n  (second (vm/ast->datoms-with-root ast)))\n\n\n(defn- resolved-var-addresses\n  \"The sequence of resolved variable references read directly off\n   resolved tuples, in evaluation order (design section 2.2): operator\n   then operands left to right, `if` test then arms, the main body first\n   then each out-of-line lambda body in discovery order, an occurrence\n   referenced twice contributing twice. A bound reference appears as\n   `[:bound depth position]`, a free reference as `[:free name]`.\n\n   Deliberately mirrors `yin.vm.debruijn-linearize/flatten-resolved`'s\n   own walk order (main sequence first, lambda bodies queued and\n   processed after) without calling it -- that function is private, and\n   this phase's job is to independently pin the same law its private\n   walk already produces, not to reuse its internals.\"\n  [get-attr root]\n  (let [addrs (atom []), bodies (atom [])]\n    (letfn [(walk\n              [e]\n              (case (get-attr e :yin/type)\n                :variable\n                (let [free (get-attr e :yin.resolved/free)]\n                  (swap! addrs conj\n                         (if (some? free)\n                           [:free free]\n                           [:bound (get-attr e :yin.resolved/depth)\n                            (get-attr e :yin.resolved/position)])))\n\n                :lambda (swap! bodies conj (get-attr e :yin/body))\n\n                :application\n                (do (walk (get-attr e :yin/operator))\n                    (run! walk (get-attr e :yin/operands)))\n\n                :dao.stream.apply/call (run! walk (get-attr e :yin/operands))\n\n                :if\n                (do (walk (get-attr e :yin/test))\n                    (walk (get-attr e :yin/consequent))\n                    (walk (get-attr e :yin/alternate)))\n\n                :stream/put\n                (do (walk (get-attr e :yin/target))\n                    (walk (get-attr e :yin/val-node)))\n\n                (:stream/cursor :stream/next :stream/close)\n                (walk (get-attr e :yin/source))\n\n                :vm/resume (walk (get-attr e :yin/val-node))\n\n                nil))]\n      (walk root)\n      (loop [i 0]\n        (when-let [body (get @bodies i)]\n          (walk body)\n          (recur (inc i))))\n      @addrs)))\n\n\n(defn- stack-image-var-addresses\n  \"The sequence of `:load-bound`/`:load-free` operands read off a\n   `:yin.debruijn.code/*` image in pc order, the same shape\n   `resolved-var-addresses` returns.\"\n  [image]\n  (into []\n        (keep (fn [t]\n                (case (nth t 0)\n                  :load-bound [:bound (nth t 1) (nth t 2)]\n                  :load-free [:free (nth t 1)]\n                  nil)))\n        image))\n\n\n(defn resolved-addresses-of\n  \"Public: R1's own three-way address-law test calls this directly\n   (never via var-quote reflection, which does not port to ClojureDart)\n   rather than duplicating this file's fixture-building code.\"\n  [ast]\n  (let [{:keys [tuples]} (dr/resolve (ast-datoms ast))\n        {:keys [get-attr root-id]} (vm/index-datoms tuples)]\n    (resolved-var-addresses get-attr root-id)))\n\n\n(defn stack-addresses-of\n  \"Public for the same reason as `resolved-addresses-of`.\"\n  [ast]\n  (stack-image-var-addresses (:image (dl/adapt (ast-datoms ast)))))\n\n\n;; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n;; B2's duplicate-parameter and shared-occurrence fixtures, reconstructed\n;; here exactly as `yin.vm.debruijn-resolve-test`/`yin.vm.debruijn-\n;; linearize-test` build them, per this lineage's own convention that\n;; each test file keeps its own copy of the AST fixture builders.\n\n(defn- v\n  [s]\n  {:type :variable, :name s})\n\n\n(defn- lam\n  [params body]\n  {:type :lambda, :params params, :body body})\n\n\n(defn- app\n  [op & args]\n  {:type :application, :operator op, :operands (vec args)})\n\n\n(defn- tail\n  [node]\n  (assoc node :tail? true))\n\n\n(def ^:private duplicate-param\n  \"`(fn [x x] x)`: `resolve-name`'s rightmost-wins duplicate-parameter\n   case, reused from B2's own named example.\"\n  (lam '[x x] (tail (v 'x))))\n\n\n(def ^:private free-variable\n  \"A body referencing a name no enclosing closure binds, reused from\n   B2's own fixture of the same name.\"\n  (lam '[x] (tail (app (v '+) (v 'x) (v 'y)))))\n\n\n(def ^:private shared-variable-body\n  (assoc (v 'x) :eid -41))\n\n\n(def ^:private shared-variable-under-two-contexts\n  \"One `:variable` entity referenced as the body of two lambdas with\n   different parameter vectors, reused from B2's own fixture of the same\n   name: resolves bound under `[x]` and free under `[y]`.\"\n  (app (v 'list) (lam '[x] shared-variable-body) (lam '[y] shared-variable-body)))\n\n\n(def ^:private shared-lambda-body\n  (assoc (lam '[z] (tail (app (v '+) (v 'z) (v 'q)))) :eid -70))\n\n\n(def ^:private shared-lambda-under-two-contexts\n  \"A `:lambda` entity referenced from two outer lambdas, reused from\n   B2's own fixture of the same name: its body's `q` resolves bound\n   under one outer context and free under the other.\"\n  (app (v 'list) (lam '[p q] shared-lambda-body) (lam '[r] shared-lambda-body)))\n\n\n(def address-law-extra-fixtures\n  \"B2's duplicate-parameter and shared-occurrence fixtures the R0 box\n   names by name, added to the reused B0 parity corpus below. Public for\n   the same reason as `resolved-addresses-of`: R1's own address-law test\n   reuses this directly, never via var-quote reflection.\"\n  {:duplicate-param duplicate-param,\n   :free-variable free-variable,\n   :shared-variable-under-two-contexts shared-variable-under-two-contexts,\n   :shared-lambda-under-two-contexts shared-lambda-under-two-contexts})\n\n\n(deftest address-law-holds-over-the-b0-parity-corpus\n  (testing \"addresses(resolve x) = addresses(lower-stack (resolve x)) for\n            every yin.vm.parity-test/corpus program\"\n    (doseq [[name ast _expected] parity/corpus]\n      (testing name\n        (is (= (resolved-addresses-of ast) (stack-addresses-of ast)))))))\n\n\n(deftest address-law-holds-over-b2s-duplicate-and-shared-occurrence-fixtures\n  (doseq [[label ast] address-law-extra-fixtures]\n    (testing label\n      (is (= (resolved-addresses-of ast) (stack-addresses-of ast))))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; 4. Pure data checks on the register descriptor's declared field list\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Design section 3's table, as data, checked for internal consistency\n;; against this phase's own operand-mapping table where that is checkable\n;; without R1's code -- this is a small part of the phase, not its\n;; center of gravity.\n\n(def register-descriptor-fields\n  \"Design section 3's field table, transcribed as data.\"\n  {:namespace :yin.debruijn.register\n   :contract-version register-contract-version\n   :source :resolved-tuples\n   :register-image :body-ranges-plus-positional-instructions\n   :scalar-encoding :b1-scalar-bytes\n   :lexical-addressing :load-bound\n   :free-addressing :load-free\n   :validation [:shape :targets :body-scope :register-bounds\n                :live-shape :live-bounds :live-tail :live-exact]\n   :lift :to-named-semantics})\n\n\n(deftest descriptor-field-list-has-every-declared-field\n  (is (= #{:namespace :contract-version :source :register-image\n           :scalar-encoding :lexical-addressing :free-addressing\n           :validation :lift}\n         (set (keys register-descriptor-fields)))))\n\n\n(deftest descriptor-contract-version-field-matches-the-frozen-constant\n  (is (= register-contract-version (:contract-version register-descriptor-fields))))\n\n\n(deftest descriptor-lexical-and-free-addressing-match-the-operand-mapping\n  (testing \"'Lexical addressing: :load-bound depth/position remains\n            explicit' agrees with the :bound branch this phase's own\n            operand-mapping table gives :variable\"\n    (is (= (:lexical-addressing register-descriptor-fields)\n           (get-in node-type->register-mapping [:variable :bound :instruction]))))\n  (testing \"'Free addressing: :load-free name remains exact' agrees with\n            the :free branch\"\n    (is (= (:free-addressing register-descriptor-fields)\n           (get-in node-type->register-mapping [:variable :free :instruction])))))\n\n\n(deftest descriptor-declared-instructions-are-in-section-four-fours-instruction-set\n  (is (contains? register-instruction-mnemonics (:lexical-addressing register-descriptor-fields)))\n  (is (contains? register-instruction-mnemonics (:free-addressing register-descriptor-fields))))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cp /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792560000000-architect-phase8-plan.report.md collab/; sed -n '2168,2260p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md; cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md | sed -n '185,265p'; rg -n -A5 -B3 '8e Old' collab/1792560000000-architect-phase8-plan.report.md; rg -n 'debruijn-(resolve|linearize|register-compile)|\\\\b(dl|rc|resolve)/|:old' src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc test/yin/vm/test_utils.cljc test/yin/vm/semantic_register/derive_test.cljc; cat src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/derive.cljc | head -100; head -65 src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/linearize.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cp /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792560000000-architect-phase8-plan.report.md collab/; sed -n '2168,2260p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md; cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md | sed -n '185,265p'; rg -n -A5 -B3 '8e Old' collab/1792560000000-architect-phase8-plan.report.md; rg -n 'debruijn-(resolve|linearize|register-compile)|\\\\b(dl|rc|resolve)/|:old' src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc test/yin/vm/test_utils.cljc test/yin/vm/semantic_register/derive_test.cljc; cat src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/derive.cljc | head -100; head -65 src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/linearize.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"### 8.4 Phase 7's entry conditions\n\n**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: phases 6 and 7a-7c now provide the entry seams; 7d reports the Stage E port.\nPhase 7's entry conditions are met: the phase-6 row carrier and frame/layout\nharness are available; the holder, rows checkpoint inspector, ledger and\nauthority use packs; free-environment retention is threaded through the\nlift; recovery format 2 is based on rows. The Stage D composition uses\nsemantic-register machines and the composition's function `:source-index`\nseam. The 7d report records the corresponding Stage E world/scenario port\nand local JVM results; 7d is still in review. Entry readiness is not\ntri-host phase acceptance: Node/Dart Stage E and the killed four-pair\nJVM/Node process matrix remain the orchestrator's gates. The owner accepts\nthe REPL custody gap until phase 8. Versions 0/1/2 remain only as direct\nlegacy codecs/tests until phase 8, without compatibility or version dispatch\nin the row holder/authority path. The two backends remain as §4.6 specifies.\n\n### 8.5 Ordering and concurrency\n\n| | needs | concurrent with |\n|---|---|---|\n| 6a | 4b stage 1 (branch) | 4b stage 2, 5a-5c, 6c-pre |\n| 6c-pre | nothing of 4b or 5 | everything |\n| 6b | 4b stage 2; 5c (scanners) | 5a, 5b, 6c-pre |\n| 6c | 5a, 5b, 6b, 6c-pre | 5c |\n\nNo file of 4b or phase 5 is edited. The old kernel file\n`debruijn/stack.cljc` is edited by 6c-pre only, for the seam; the\nregister kernel is not edited. `handoff.cljc` and the old harness are\nnot edited. **Amended after 6c (2026-10-13):** 6c proper held to this:\nit created `layout.cljc` and `layout_test.cljc`, extended the 6b harness\nfile and the store-write audit's allowlist, and edited no kernel, no\n`rows.cljc` and no 4b or phase-5 file. The one further kernel edit this\ndesign named, the retained-FFI response-pair seam of both de Bruijn\nkernels (§4.3, 6c-post-D2), was rejected by the owner (§11, 6c Q2); this\ndesign names no kernel edit beyond 6c-pre (**Amended 2026-10-13\n(backends)**: the backends' fragment is not widened, §4.6).\n\n---\n\n## 9. Verification honesty\n\n- **Hand-derived numbers.** `saved(4, 2) = {1}` and `L(5)` on the worked\n  example are the phase-3 tests' (`analysis_test.cljc:131-139`). The\n  stack slices `[f]` at the inner call of `(f (g x) y)` and at `(g)` in\n  `(f (if c (g) 2) y)` were derived twice from the §4.3 walk and agreed\n  with the reviewer's derivation; the register `phys(1) = 0`, `phys(2) =\n  3` follow from phase-5 §6.3 row 2, whose pinned `live` at pc 3 is `[0]`\n  (`{T0,T3}` minus the destination `T3`), the same set §4.2 step 4 and\n  §5.6 item 3 compute by subtracting the virtual `rd 2` before mapping.\n  The multi-frame bases in 6c item 6 (`2, 4` for the expression alone;\n  `0, 0, 0, 2, 4` as run under the harness's bindings) were derived\n  twice from the kernel's call arm (`stack.cljc:716-733`, base = height\n  after popping callee and operands); the first pass forgot the pushed\n  operator, and revision 2's lowering rule used the height **before**\n  each slice, which the round-2 review caught and §4.3 now states as\n  cumulative through the slice. Nothing was run.\n- **Claims that are checks, not theorems**: the stack operand slice\n  equals the saved set at non-tail safepoints (§5.6 item 5); the non-push\n  H ↔ A correspondence (item 4); `virt` single-valued at reachable\n  boundaries (item 3); layout `E` = evaluator `E` (item 6); the tail\n  lowering's unused fields are unobservable (item 8); the slices\n  partition the stack (item 9).\n- **What is not claimed**: that kernel state round-trips (shadowed outer\n  positional values are nil-filled; the law is on canonical frames);\n  that every kernel reconstructs canonical frames (two are named; the\n  walker is excluded); that today's stack kernel delivers a tail effect\n  through `K` (it does not; 6c-pre adds it).\n- **Private access.** Every function a test names is public; no `#'`.\n- **Unverified.** 4b stage 2 is not landed: rows 3-4, 7-12, 17, 24 are\n  written against the evaluator design's §1.4-1.6 shapes and the stage-1\n  branch and must be re-read when stage 2 lands. The seam of §4.3 is a\n  specification of a change to `stack.cljc`, not a reading of code that\n  exists. **Amended after 6b (2026-10-13):** 4b stage 2 landed before\n  6b, and rows 3-4, 7-12, 17 and 24 now run on the reference lane (89\n  labelled row × segment executions, 85 distinct builder/wrapper setups\n  since rows 3 and 24 share one builder by design); rows 10-11 were\n  re-read and corrected (§5.2, B1). The 6b harness ran on the JVM only;\n  Node and Dart execution of `rows.cljc`'s transport and the new harness\n  file remain the three-host gate of §8.2 item 9, not established by the\n  reviews.\n- **Amended after 6c (2026-10-13).** 6c-pre landed (`296f75fd`) and the\n  stack kernel delivers a tail effect through `K`; §4.3's seam is now a\n  reading of code. The layouts' counts are the tests' own assertions,\n  run on the JVM by the engineer and by codex (quick: 60 tests, 3,615\n  assertions; slow: 4 tests, 2,419 assertions, before the fix round):\n  law 4 on 16 bare and 61 full cases over three distinct lanes, law 5 on\n  92 and 350 transfers, 128 `E` comparisons, four tail cases, more than\n  twenty slice partitions and more than fifty reachable boundaries. The\n  rulings' claims that are checks, not theorems: that C holds an\n  ambiguous dead preimage (asserted, §8.3 item 12); that the alpha pair\n  derives one R and one H (asserted, item 14). **What this document has\n  no record of:** a Node or Dart run of `layout.cljc` and the 6c tests.\n  Codex read the pinned ClojureDart source and found `satisfies?`,\n## Test tiers: quick and slow\n\nThe suite has two tiers so that slow tests run less often than quick ones\n(owner decision, 2026-10-12).\n\n- **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\n  `clojure -M:test -e :slow` on the JVM; the Node and Dart lanes skip the\n  guarded bodies (`dao.test-slow/guard`) unless `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`.\n- **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\n  `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>` on the JVM.\n- **The tagging rule** is the one above (\"When to tag a test slow\"): a deftest that\n  takes about 5 s or more run alone. On 2026-10-12 every JVM test namespace and every\n  deftest in the 22 slowest namespaces were timed on a 48-core Linux server, and the\n  **63 deftests at 5 s or more** were tagged: `^:slow` on the deftest, plus the guard\n  wrapper in `.cljc` files. 37 are guarded, 26 are `^:slow` only (`.clj` files and\n  `:clj` reader-conditional branches). Those 22 namespaces now hold **168 slow tests**\n  in all (some were already tagged). Per-test times are not in the repo: measure again\n  with the method below before changing the cut.\n- **Cadence:** the quick tier at every checkpoint (G1) and landing (G2, G3); the slow\n  tier before merging a big change and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track\n  (the existing G4). There is no nightly run.\n\nMeasured (2026-10-12, origin master plus the tags, Linux servers; see \"Remote test\nservers\" for how to run them):\n\n| Run | Time |\n|---|---|\n| Full JVM lane, one process, before tagging (shiva) | 70 min |\n| Full JVM lane, sharded by subsystem, before tagging (jupiter, 13 shards, 8 at a time) | 46.5 min, set by `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` |\n| **Quick tier, sharded by subsystem (jupiter, same 13 shards)** | **5 min 3 s**, 4055 tests, all shards exit 0; longest shards `yang.python` 253 s and `yin.vm.ucf` 204 s |\n| **Slow tier (shiva, one JVM per namespace, 6 at a time)** | **53 min**, 22 namespaces, 168 tests, all exit 0; longest `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` 3183 s (about 1580 s alone on the laptop) |\n\nThe quick tier has exactly 63 fewer tests than the untagged suite (4118 to 4055).\nSome quick parts are still 25 to 69 s when a namespace is run alone (`e2e-c2`,\n`prelude-parity`, `int-ops`, `holder.driver`, `compose-rows`): many tests each just\nunder the 5 s cut (the Python e2e per-test floor is about 2 to 3 s), not shared setup.\nA lower cut would shrink the quick tier further and move more tests to the slow tier.\n\n**How the per-test times were measured.** To time every deftest of a namespace alone,\nrun it without the test runner's alias main (the `:test` alias's `-m cognitect.test-runner`\notherwise swallows `-e`, and every JVM runs the whole suite):\n\n```sh\nNS=<namespace> clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:t {:extra-paths [\"test\"]}}}' -M:t timing.clj\n# timing.clj: require the namespace, for each deftest var that is not ^:slow:\n#   (binding [clojure.test/*test-out* (java.io.StringWriter.)] (clojure.test/test-vars [v]))\n# and print the elapsed nanoseconds. One-off setup (a `use-fixtures :once` or a parser\n# load) is paid by the first test in file order; check before tagging that test alone.\n```\n\n**Sharding the JVM lane by subsystem** (the quick tier; run on a server, see below). The\nshards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\ntests, so each subsystem is one JVM; the script also checks that the shards cover every\nJVM test namespace under `test/`:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Usage: shard-jvm.sh <clone> [parallel]  -- JVM lane sharded by subsystem (fast tests, -e :slow)\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nPAR=\"${2:-8}\"\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nOUT=\"$1/target-ci/shards\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\necho \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" | tee \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nnpm ci --silent >\"$OUT/npm.log\" 2>&1                   && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$OUT/build-node.log\" 2>&1     && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nbb gen:python-antlr >\"$OUT/antlr.log\" 2>&1             && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nSUBS=\"yang.python yin.repl yin.vm yin.vm.ucf dao.stream dao.jing dao.gui dao.space demo dao.data dao.postgraphics yang.base dao.base\"\n: > \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\nfor s in $SUBS; do\n  bb test:sub:list \"$s\" 2>/dev/null | awk \"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\"\\\");print}\" > \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"\n  cat \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\" >> \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\n  echo \"$s $(wc -l < \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\")\" >> \"$OUT/plan.txt\"\ndone\n# coverage: every JVM test namespace under test/ vs the union of the shards\nfind test -type f \\( -name \"*_test.clj\" -o -name \"*_test.cljc\" \\) | sed -e \"s#^test/##\" -e \"s#\\.cljc\\?\\$##\" -e \"s#/#.#g\" -e \"s#_#-#g\" | sort -u > \"$OUT/all.txt\"\nsort -u \"$OUT/covered.txt\" > \"$OUT/covered.sorted\"\ncomm -23 \"$OUT/all.txt\" \"$OUT/covered.sorted\" > \"$OUT/ns-uncovered.txt\"\n195-| **8b BLAKE3** | `debruijn_code.cljc:138, :548, :564`, `debruijn_register_code.cljc:200, :258, :269`, `debruijn.cljc:139-140, :966, :1028` + docstrings; `debruijn_test.cljc:136, :933, :1300-1306`; re-pin 8a's goldens once more (or merge 8a+8b so goldens move once: **recommend merging 8a and 8b into one slice \"8ab\"** so every H/R constant regenerates exactly once) | 8a | same as 8a plus `debruijn-test/*`, `pipeline-test/*`; descriptor-hash recomputed by `(jing/blake3 (dc/encode-scalar <printed descriptor>))` | 3 | 0.5 d | 2 |\n196-| **8c Production pipeline to A** | `linker.cljc:46-52` requires, `:695-731` one `semantic-format` (v4 scanners), `:2159-2177` `relowered`, `:2252-2320` `checked-derivation` (de Bruijn input = A), `:2457-2565` pairing paths; `publish.cljc:5-17, :159-213`; `ledger.cljc:40-50, :194-204`; `vm.cljc:266-269` `\"v4\"`, `:1755` footprint key; `ucf.cljc:47-68`; `completion.cljc:206, :256` (Q5); `interpreter.cljc:84-93`; `repl.cljc:27-41, :143-156, :224-240`; `rows.cljc:2118`, `semantic_register.cljc:410-456` keyword to `:yin.semantic/code` (so the keyword moves once, here, not in 8f); tests `linker_test`, `linker_manifest_test:740`, `linker_require_test`, `linker/publish_test`, `repl/serve_test:446-452`, `semantic_register/link_test`, `repl_test`, `ucf_test` (v4 vectors), `ledger_test` | 8ab | `linker-test/*` incl. `pinned-identities-are-host-independent`, `verify-same-root-pairing` tests `:1969-1980`, `linker-manifest-test/*`, `linker-require-test/*`, `publish-test/*` (manifest shape: contracts `\"v4\"`, three derivation records with de Bruijn input = A, index keyed by format), `serve-test/the-composition-registers-the-v4-format-record` (6 records), `repl-test/*`, `dht_process_test`, `remote_transfer_test`, `cross_host_transfer_test`; `derive_test/c4-execution-oracle` still green | 3 (JVM + Node REPL peer + Dart peer are all involved) | 3 d | 3-4 |\n197-| **8d Old transport out** | delete `handoff.cljc`, `checkpoint.cljc`, 15 test files, `lift_support.cljc`, `v2_support.cljc`, `checkpoint_fixtures.cljc`, 3 fixtures + minter; first move borrowed helpers into `rows_support.cljc` (new `frame_test`, `layout_test`, `semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test` requires rewired); `int_heap_test.cljc:241`, `int_contract_test.cljc:483` to the holder path (C4 files: Q1) | none (can run concurrently with 8ab/8c) | `rows-test/*`, `rows-checkpoint-test/*`, `compose-rows-test/*` (41), `fencing-rows-test`, `stage-e-rows-test`, `stage_e_process_test`, `holder/*`, `authority/*`, `semantic-register/safepoint-harness-test/*` (26), `frame-test/*` (10), `layout-test/*` (12); one new refusal row for a version-0/2 header | 3 | 1.5 d | 2-3 |\n198:| **8e Old lowerers out** | delete `debruijn_resolve.cljc`, `debruijn_linearize.cljc`, `debruijn_register_compile.cljc`, their 3 tests, `debruijn_register_benchmark_test`; port the contract facts of `debruijn_vm_contract_test`/`debruijn_register_contract_test` to `debruijn_code_test`/`debruijn_register_code_test`, delete the rest; `test_utils.cljc:24, :200-210` (`resolved-of` becomes `derive` over the projected vector); `derive_test` `:old` legs out (Q10); every test that builds a kernel through `dl/adapt`/`rc/adapt` (37 files, the 10 C4 files among them: Q1) switches to `derive/stack-image`/`register-image` over `(:vector (sr-linearize/project ast))` | 8c (production no longer calls them) | the full `yin.vm` subsystem quick tier; `derive-test/*` incl. `c4-execution-oracle` (`--slow`); `debruijn/register_test`, `debruijn/stack_*_test`; kondo clean (`clj -M:kondo`) with no unresolved namespace | 3 | 2 d | 2-3 |\n199-| **8f Old kernel out, rename, C4 gate** | delete `semantic.cljc`, `linearize.cljc`, `code.cljc` (what remains after 8c) and `semantic_test`, `linearize_test`, `code_test`; port `semantic_engine_test`, `semantic_ffi_test`, `semantic_stream_observer_test` to the new kernel; commit 1 `git mv` `semantic_register*` to `semantic*` and `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/` to `test/yin/vm/semantic/`; commit 2 rewrite the 56 requirers (`vm-load-program` to `load-ast`/`load-vector`); demos (`continuation_handoff.cljc`, `continuation_stream.cljs`, `register_bench_cljd.cljd`, `yin_vm_bench`) ported; `linked_harness.cljc:105-120`; C4 goldens regenerated and literal pins verified (Q1, Q8); coverage audit list | 8c, 8d, 8e | **phase gate**: `bb test:all` three hosts (slow tier), `bb test:sub yang.python --slow` (C4 suite: `import_test`, `linked_prelude_test`, `linked_safepoint_test`, `safepoint_test`, `dht_import_test`, `e2e_test`, `e2e_c2_test`, `prelude_parity_test`) over newly published images = the install-child gate of derivations §6.1.4 (c); `repl-test/*`, `dht_process_test`, `store_write_audit_test` (allowlist touches); deftest-count audit per Q7; kondo clean; `lib/cljd-out` cleaned before the Dart lane | 3 + shiva/jupiter for the slow tier | 3 d | 3-4 |\n200-| **8g Documentation** | the 13 design docs, `yin.vm.semantic.md` fate (Q6), `ucf-revisions.md` record, `linker.md` §3, `build-n-test.md` if needed, the frozen design's §10 item 8 marked complete, `derivations.md` §7.4-8.3 and `continuation.md` §8.4 statuses, `debruijn.*.md` SHA-256 prose | 8f | Architect doc review; `grep -r 'yin.vm.semantic-register' docs src test` returns only historical/dated statements; `grep -rn 'sha256' src/cljc/yin/vm` returns none | n/a | 1.5 d | 2 |\n201-\n202-Ordering and concurrency:\n203-\ntest/yin/vm/test_utils.cljc:24:            [yin.vm.debruijn-resolve :as resolve]\ntest/yin/vm/test_utils.cljc:200:  \"`(resolve/resolve (vm/ast->datoms ast))`, kept for the last `ast` by\ntest/yin/vm/test_utils.cljc:207:      (let [v (resolve/resolve (vm/ast->datoms ast))]\ntest/yin/vm/semantic_register/derive_test.cljc:14:            [yin.vm.debruijn-register-compile :as rc]\ntest/yin/vm/semantic_register/derive_test.cljc:23:            [yin.vm.debruijn-resolve :as dr]\ntest/yin/vm/semantic_register/derive_test.cljc:24:            [yin.vm.debruijn-linearize :as dl]\ntest/yin/vm/semantic_register/derive_test.cljc:155:    (let [image (:image (if (= pipeline :old)\ntest/yin/vm/semantic_register/derive_test.cljc:156:                          (dl/adapt (vm/ast->datoms ast))\ntest/yin/vm/semantic_register/derive_test.cljc:334:      (let [old (stack-run :old ast opts) new (stack-run :new ast opts)]\ntest/yin/vm/semantic_register/derive_test.cljc:341:      (let [old (stack-run :old ast {}) new (stack-run :new ast {})]\ntest/yin/vm/semantic_register/derive_test.cljc:350:            old (stack-run :old ast opts) new (stack-run :new ast opts)]\ntest/yin/vm/semantic_register/derive_test.cljc:443:    (let [image (if (= pipeline :old) (:image (rc/adapt (vm/ast->datoms ast)))\ntest/yin/vm/semantic_register/derive_test.cljc:561:      (let [old (register-run :old ast opts) new (register-run :new ast opts)]\ntest/yin/vm/semantic_register/derive_test.cljc:566:      (let [old (register-run :old ast {}) new (register-run :new ast {})]\ntest/yin/vm/semantic_register/derive_test.cljc:572:            old (register-run :old ast opts) new (register-run :new ast opts)]\ntest/yin/vm/semantic_register/derive_test.cljc:620:                (dvm/create-vm (:image (dl/adapt (vm/ast->datoms ast)))\ntest/yin/vm/semantic_register/derive_test.cljc:626:                   (rvm/create-vm (:image (rc/adapt (vm/ast->datoms ast)))\ntest/yin/vm/semantic_register/derive_test.cljc:676:                      old (observe :old) new (observe :new)]\ntest/yin/vm/semantic_register/derive_test.cljc:682:            (let [old (c4-task family :old ast source) new (c4-task family :new ast source)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc:855:             \"(yin.vm.debruijn-register-compile/adapt), then load and run the \"\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc:45:   use; B2 (`yin.vm.debruijn-linearize`) produces real images against this\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc:922:             \"The de Bruijn kernel executes raw instruction vectors, not AST: lower and adapt it first (yin.vm.debruijn-linearize/adapt), then load and run the resulting image\"\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc:14:   layout `yin.vm.debruijn-linearize/lower-stack` already uses for the\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc:294:;; Used by both `yin.vm.debruijn-register-compile/lower-register`, to\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc:299:;; (`yin.vm.debruijn-register-compile/allocate-temp!`/`free-temp!`) is a\n(ns yin.vm.semantic-register.derive\n  \"Pure native derivations from the admitted semantic register vector A.\"\n  (:refer-clojure :exclude [resolve])\n  (:require [yin.vm.semantic-register.code :as code]\n            [yin.vm.debruijn :as db]\n            [yin.vm.semantic-register.analysis :as analysis]\n            [yin.vm.debruijn-register-code :as rcode]))\n\n\n(defn- chains\n  [v bodies]\n  (letfn [(chain\n            [i]\n            (if-let [owner (:owner (nth bodies i))]\n              (into [(nth (nth v owner) 2)]\n                    (chain (code/body-index bodies owner)))\n              []))]\n    (mapv chain (range (count bodies)))))\n\n\n(defn resolve\n  \"Rewrite only variables and closure binders. The result is an unhashed\n   stage value; pcs and virtual registers are preserved.\"\n  [v]\n  (let [bodies (code/parse v)\n        frames (chains v bodies)\n        names (volatile! {})]\n    {:vector\n     (mapv (fn [pc]\n             (let [[op rd x y :as t] (nth v pc)]\n               (case op\n                 :var (let [{:keys [bound free]}\n                            (db/resolve-name (nth frames (code/body-index bodies pc)) x)]\n                        (if bound\n                          (do (vswap! names assoc pc x)\n                              (into [:load-bound rd] bound))\n                          [:load-free rd free]))\n                 :closure [:closure rd (count x) y]\n                 t)))\n           (range (count v)))\n     :bodies bodies\n     :names @names}))\n\n\n(defn addresses\n  \"Lexical references in pc order, from A, a resolved vector or a native\n   instruction vector (register or stack). Returns a vector, no identity.\"\n  [v]\n  (let [v (if (map? v) (or (:vector v) (:instructions v)) v)\n        v (if (some #(= :var (first %)) v) (:vector (resolve v)) v)]\n    (into []\n          (keep (fn [t]\n                  (case (first t)\n                    :load-bound (into [:bound] (subvec t (- (count t) 2)))\n                    :load-free [:free (peek t)]\n                    nil)))\n          v)))\n\n\n(defn resolved-addresses\n  \"Read lexical references from the old resolver's occurrence tuples.\n   Walk main then queued lambda bodies; definitions visit only their value.\n   This diagnostic reader never lowers or assigns an identity.\"\n  [{:keys [tuples]}]\n  (let [attrs (reduce (fn [m [e a v]] (assoc-in m [e a] v)) {} tuples)\n        root (first (keep (fn [[e m]] (when (:yin/root m) e)) attrs))\n        queue (volatile! [])\n        out (volatile! [])]\n    (letfn [(walk\n              [e]\n              (let [m (get attrs e)\n                    child (fn [k] (walk (get m k)))]\n                (case (:yin/type m)\n                  :variable (vswap! out conj\n                                    (if (contains? m :yin.resolved/free)\n                                      [:free (:yin.resolved/free m)]\n                                      [:bound (:yin.resolved/depth m)\n                                       (:yin.resolved/position m)]))\n                  :lambda (vswap! queue conj (:yin/body m))\n                  :application\n                  (let [op (get attrs (:yin/operator m))\n                        args (:yin/operands m)]\n                    (if (= 'yin/def (:yin.resolved/free op))\n                      (walk (nth args 1))\n                      (do (child :yin/operator) (doseq [a args] (walk a)))))\n                  :if (doseq [k [:yin/test :yin/consequent :yin/alternate]] (child k))\n                  :stream/put (do (child :yin/target) (child :yin/val-node))\n                  (:stream/cursor :stream/next :stream/close) (child :yin/source)\n                  :dao.stream.apply/call (doseq [a (:yin/operands m)] (walk a))\n                  :vm/resume (child :yin/val-node)\n                  nil)))]\n      (walk root)\n      (loop [i 0]\n        (when-let [e (get @queue i)] (walk e) (recur (inc i))))\n      @out)))\n\n\n(defn stack-image\n  \"Stackify A's parsed expressions, placing closure bodies in FIFO order.\n   Labels and diagnostics are derived solely from A.\"\n(ns yin.vm.semantic-register.linearize\n  \"`project`: the Universal AST → the register-shaped canonical vector\n   (`docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md` §3.3).\n\n   One flattening, two lanes, as `yin.vm.linearize` has them: the datom\n   lane reads `:yin/*` datoms through an entity index, the row lane reads\n   code-as-tuples rows (§5.1) by the grammar table's positions. Both hand\n   the same walk a node reader, so the order is shared by construction:\n   operator, then operands left to right; a conditional's test,\n   consequent, alternate; occurrences expanded positionally; saturated\n   defaults; lambda bodies out of line after the main sequence, in one\n   FIFO queue that bodies discovered inside bodies join at the back.\n\n   Registers are body-local and minted pre-order: a destination is minted\n   when its expression is entered, before its children; a conditional's\n   arms are lowered into the conditional's destination and mint nothing\n   themselves; a `:resume` mints like every other expression though no\n   instruction names its id as a destination. No moves, folds or\n   rewrites.\n\n   Provenance is a side table beside the vector, never inside it; the\n   code address is `ucf/code-address` of the vector.\"\n  (:require [yin.vm :as vm]\n            [yin.vm.ucf :as ucf]))\n\n\n(def unsupported\n  \"Node types outside the shared supported AST vocabulary, rejected at\n   projection naming the node.\"\n  #{:yin/macro-expand :vm/store-update})\n\n\n(def ^:private slot-position\n  \"`[tag field]` → the field's row position (§2.5: id 0, tag 1, first\n   slot 2), from `vm/semantic-bytecode-grammar`. Provenance path steps\n   are these positions in both lanes.\"\n  (reduce-kv (fn [m tag slots]\n               (reduce (fn [m i]\n                         (assoc m [tag (first (nth slots i))] (+ 2 i)))\n                       m\n                       (range (count slots))))\n             {}\n             vm/semantic-bytecode-grammar))\n\n\n(def ^:private defaults\n  \"§2.4 / UCF §7.3.2 saturation, applied before lowering. The row lane's\n   rows are already saturated; the datom lane's facts may omit these.\"\n  {[:vm/gensym :prefix] \"id\",\n   [:stream/make :buffer] vm/default-stream-capacity,\n   [:application :operands] [],\n   [:dao.stream.apply/call :operands] []})\n\n\n(defn- flatten-tree\n  \"The §3.3 walk from `root` over `reader`, `{:tag (fn [id]) :slot (fn\n   [id field]) :check (fn [id])}`. `:check` runs on entering each lowered\n   node and throws to refuse it. Returns `{:code [tuple …] :sources [[id\n   path] …]}`, one source per pc, labels resolved to pcs.\"\n  [{:keys [tag slot check]} root]\n  (let [code (volatile! [])\n        sources (volatile! [])\n        labels (volatile! {})\n        bodies (volatile! [])\n        label-count (volatile! 0)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
